<h2>A case that outlived its era</h2>
<p>The FIR was registered at P.S. Ashok Vihar in April 1999; the trial court convicted the husband in 2003, sentencing him to life imprisonment under Section 302 IPC and three years under Section 498A. The appeal then waited through two decades of docket — by the time it was decided, the IPC itself had been replaced by the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita for new cases. The appeal, of course, remained governed by the law under which the offence was committed, a reminder that IPC-era jurisprudence will govern Delhi's criminal appeal rolls for years to come.</p>

<h2>The sequence that decided the appeal</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Admission — the MLC</strong><p>Pooja, with 100% burns, tells the doctor she was sleeping and does not know how she caught fire. The doctor records her as conscious and oriented.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Relatives arrive</strong><p>Her family reaches the hospital in the course of the day.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Statement to the IO</strong><p>A second account is recorded by the investigating officer.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Statement to the SDM</strong><p>At about 5:30 pm she tells the SDM that her husband poured kerosene on her and set her on fire after a quarrel. She dies the next day.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The trial court had treated the SDM statement as the operative dying declaration. The Division Bench inverted the analysis: the earliest statement, closest in time to the incident and before any opportunity for outside influence, exculpated the accused — and the prosecution never explained the transformation, nor proved that the declarant's condition at the later statements permitted a reliable account. No record showed what treatment or medication she had received in the interval.</p>

<h2>The supporting fabric</h2>
<p>The Bench did not rest on the declarations alone. The neighbour's evidence placed the husband as arriving after the fire began. His own burns were consistent with rescue, not assault. The dowry-harassment case under Section 498A fared no better: the victim's letters contained no complaint of harassment, and the family's versions diverged. Most strikingly, the couple's young son — an eyewitness by location, himself burnt in the incident — was never produced, and the Court drew an adverse inference from the omission. The cumulative picture fell short of proof beyond reasonable doubt, and the appellant was acquitted on both counts.</p>

<h2>What the judgment teaches</h2>
<p>The law of dying declarations is deceptively simple: a truthful, voluntary declaration by a fit declarant can found a conviction without corroboration. The practical difficulties are all in the plural. Multiple declarations are the norm in burn cases — hospital history, police statement, SDM statement — and they are recorded under rapidly changing medical and social conditions. Three disciplines follow for investigators and prosecutors: record fitness contemporaneously through the treating doctor at every stage; document who had access to the declarant between statements; and treat the earliest account as the anchor whose abandonment must be explained, not ignored.</p>
<p>For the defence, the case is a template for cross-examination in multi-declaration trials: fix the timeline, extract the treatment record, identify the arrivals at the hospital, and measure each declaration against the first. And for everyone involved in criminal appeals, the twenty-three-year interval is its own commentary — the appellant served the appeal period on bail, and the bond now holds for three months against any further challenge.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This report is based on the judgment of the Delhi High Court in Prithivi Raj v. State (NCT of Delhi), CRL.A. 837/2003, pronounced on 14 October 2026. Dying declarations in post-2024 cases are dealt with under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, which carries forward the substance of Section 32(1) of the Evidence Act, 1872.</p>
</div>
